179 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H2
/P 54 0 R
/T (Agrivoltaics reshapes the economic logic of land use by combining two revenue streams on the same parcel. Figure 6 compares the economic implications of conventional ground-mounted PV \(GMPV\) and agrivoltaics, showing higher investment is needed to enable dual-land use particularly for mounting structures and surface preparation and installation.)
/E (Agrivoltaics reshapes the economic logic of land use by combining two revenue streams on the same parcel. Figure 6 compares the economic implications of conventional ground-mounted PV \(GMPV\) and agrivoltaics, showing higher investment is needed to enable dual-land use particularly for mounting structures and surface preparation and installation.)
/Pg 13 0 R
/K [8]
>>
endobj
180 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H2
/P 54 0 R
/T (Figure 7 illustrates typical revenue shares across different agrivoltaic applications, highlighting that electricity generation often contributes the majority of income, while agricultural value varies strongly by crop type, market structure, and system design. These distributions emphasise the need for regulation that protects meaningful agricultural activity and avoids designs where farming becomes marginal compared to energy production.)
/E (Figure 7 illustrates typical revenue shares across different agrivoltaic applications, highlighting that electricity generation often contributes the majority of income, while agricultural value varies strongly by crop type, market structure, and system design. These distributions emphasise the need for regulation that protects meaningful agricultural activity and avoids designs where farming becomes marginal compared to energy production.)
/Pg 13 0 R
/K [9]
>>
endobj
181 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 54 0 R
/E (Figure 7: Revenue splits of agriculture and PV production according to Trommsdorff et al. \(2024\). Coloured lines indicate the variations of considered countries. Agriculture data: FAO Stat.; PV data: Solar GIS.)
/Pg 13 0 R
/K [10]
>>
